# Route-backed inspection and return-state contract

## 1. Two presentations, one content record

A detail view has a real canonical route and a shared renderer. An ordinary in-essay click may render it in the overlay host while keeping the essay mounted. A copied link, direct arrival, refreshed deep URL, modifier click or no-JS visit renders the standalone route.

Proposed routes (check collisions with the current Astro catch-all first):

```text
/essays/<post>/
/essays/<post>/threads/<thread>/<beat>/
/essays/<post>/evidence/
/essays/<post>/evidence/<receipt>/
/essays/<post>/figures/<figure>/
```

Prototype equivalents are actual sibling HTML files such as `thread-use.html` and `evidence-drift-check.html`. They are not production route names. Every prototype detail page includes static content, not a JavaScript-only empty shell.

Never put return scroll coordinates or private source data into a public share URL. A copy-link action gives the canonical standalone URL. The origin/return token is private session state, not authority or an authentication token.

## 2. State dimensions

```ts
type ReaderLocation = { sectionId: string; sceneId: string | null };
type PreviewState = { heldSceneId: string | null; dockChoice: 'auto'|'open'|'closed' };
type FocusView = { kind: 'thread'|'evidence'|'figure'|'contents'; id: string };
type ReturnSnapshot = {
  entryKey: string; url: string; articleRevision: string;
  x: number; y: number;
  anchorId: string | null; offsetWithinAnchor: number;
  focusTargetId: string | null;
  openDisclosureIds: string[];
  reader: ReaderLocation; preview: PreviewState;
  dismissedNoteIds: string[];
};
```

Reading position, dock preview, focus selection and browser navigation are **not one variable**. The user can read Drift while holding Use; opening a focus view does not move the underlying essay to that preview.

## 3. Transition table

| Action | Document | History | Return behavior |
|---|---|---|---|
| Scroll article | Update section rule/outline/context only | No push | No focus move |
| Open Thread from essay | Preserve essay DOM; show Thread Focus | Push one owned detail entry | Root snapshot captured before lock |
| Select another beat within modal | Replace focused content only | Replace owned detail entry | Original essay snapshot unchanged |
| Open Evidence from Thread | Same dialog host, child logical state | Push child entry | Parent beat, pane scroll, disclosures retained |
| Open Figure from Thread/Evidence | Same dialog host, figure state | Push child entry | Returns to immediate parent, not top of essay |
| Escape / close current view | Remove one logical level | Back only within verified owned history | Restore parent; root if no parent |
| Back to essay | Exit complete owned inspection chain | Traverse known owned depth only | Original snapshot, not selected beat anchor |
| Read this scene in essay | Close focus then deliberate jump | Explicit anchor navigation | Chosen scene, not old reading point |
| Browser Back / Forward | Reconcile with owned entries | No extra push | Reopen corresponding view or restore root |
| Copy/open direct detail URL | Standalone server-rendered page | Ordinary navigation | “Read in essay” to related anchor |
| Refresh on detail URL | Standalone page | Keep URL | No claim that a mounted background survives |
| JS/navigation failure | Follow real href or local dialog fallback | Do not trap user | Standard page/anchor remains reachable |

## 4. History ownership

`pushState` changes the address/history but does not load the target resource. Therefore that URL must independently resolve on refresh. `pushState` and `replaceState` do not dispatch `popstate`; the controller must update its view directly on those operations and respond separately to browser traversal [MDN-01, MDN-02].

Merge namespaced state into `history.state`; do not replace unrelated application data. Include a per-document/session token and opaque entry keys. Never use `history.length > 1` as proof that Back is safe. Do not invoke `history.back()` or `history.go(-n)` unless the controller can demonstrate a contiguous chain of its own entries. Direct arrivals always get a deterministic “Read in essay” route instead.

One controller owns this route group. Default recommendation: render reading routes without Astro ClientRouter and use normal links for entry/exit. Astro's ordinary default is full-page navigation [ASTRO-01]. This is a bounded choice for the reading routes, not a mandate to remove ClientRouter from the whole site.

If the actual site requires ClientRouter on these routes, integration is a separate mandatory task: use router lifecycle/loader/swap hooks and one route state owner. Do not paste the standalone prototype controller alongside ClientRouter's `popstate` handler. Astro's `astro:after-swap` / `astro:page-load` lifecycle requires explicit setup and cleanup [ASTRO-01].

## 5. Return-position algorithm

1. Before opening: capture URL, x/y, nearest stable block anchor and relative offset, active element, disclosure states and reader preferences.
2. Lock the background while preserving its exact DOM. Use tested body-scroll locking, not only an `overflow:hidden` assumption on iOS.
3. Keep focus inside the active dialog. Suspend passive reading/context changes.
4. On return to the same mounted document: restore body styles and original x/y; then focus the original trigger with `preventScroll`.
5. If content/viewport changed, prefer anchor + relative offset over an old pixel coordinate; clamp to document bounds. Wait for relevant layout-affecting images/fonts before final correction. Never smooth-scroll the entire article during restoration.
6. If the trigger no longer exists, focus the related heading, then article heading as last fallback.
7. On direct URL or refreshed document, render standalone. An optional session-backed “Resume saved position” may be added later only after matching origin/revision/entry and testing; it is not MVP behavior.

Only one modal DOM node is open at a time. A logical parent stack is permitted. Store each parent's scroll and local expansion state before replacing the host. Restoring an evidence view must restore open receipts, not recreate a collapsed set at the top.

## 6. Focus and interaction

Opening a substantial detail view focuses a programmatically focusable title near the start. No giant `aria-describedby` wrapping all structured content. Tab stays inside the modal. Escape and visible close are equivalent one-level return actions. A separate “Back to essay” exits the full inspection chain. For a direct route, there is no modal trap and no simulated prior reading state [W3C-01].

Opening a focus view from another focus view requires an explicit click. No automatic dialog from scrolling, no focus moves during reading, no auto-open receipt and no auto-swapping images. Backdrop clicks may close Figure Viewer; for full Thread/Evidence views, keep the default conservative (visible Close or Escape), avoiding accidental dismissal during reading.

Preserve ordinary anchors and modifier semantics. `target="_blank"` is used only by explicitly labeled new-tab actions. Raw source/external links may follow the site's normal policy; opening a new tab is not the primary place-preservation strategy.

## 7. Freshness, URLs and public data

Stable receipt IDs do not prove sources. Only source records populate public capture links. Sanitize before bundling content into HTML, JSON or static routes. A collapsed disclosure or modal is not access control. Do not ship internal IPs, credentials, private tracker IDs or unapproved raw logs in a hidden component.

An inspection route represents one view of public content, not a new published essay. Define canonical links and metadata accordingly; use actual content type and relationship to parent. Do not manufacture a publication date merely because a route was generated. Check indexing policy with the site's owner.

## 8. Prototype validation boundary

The current environment's browser policy blocked navigation to local HTTP and file URLs. Rendering and interaction checks use Chromium `set_content` on the supplied self-contained HTML. The prototype handles unavailable History API URL mutation by retaining an in-document dialog stack. This fallback was exercised; it is **not** a successful hosted Back/Forward test.

Hosted route refresh, real URL traversal, browser Forward, Safari/iOS scroll restoration and ClientRouter integration remain mandatory local tests. The handoff includes their acceptance matrix rather than claiming they passed here.
