src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:16:whyItMatters: "Agentic handoffs fail silently when no durable object binds intent, evidence, and decision authority together; teams discover the gap only after a chain of confident-looking work turns out to answer the wrong question."
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:17:leaderTakeaway: "Make the Feature Contract the object of record, not the prompt or the final summary. Require explicit acceptance criteria, named evidence obligations, and a named decision authority before execution begins, and treat any change to those terms as a visible amendment rather than an implementation choice."
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:35:The research agent might have answered a subtly different question. The implementation agent might have interpreted "fixed" as "the test passes," while the product leader meant "the failure mode cannot recur." The reviewer could have examined the change without seeing the evidence behind the diagnosis. Nobody may know which source supported which claim, which acceptance criteria were binding, or who had authority to decide that the work was complete.
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:39:"Unauthenticated" here does not mean that the agents lacked cryptographic identities. It means that the handoff did not carry an authoritative relationship among intent, claims, evidence, acceptance criteria, and decision rights. Each participant received a description of the work, but no durable object proved what the work was supposed to accomplish.
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:41:The problem here isn't weak generation: it's broken continuity. Naming this failure mode is not a claim that <Term id="contract-as-spec">**contract-as-spec**</Term> solves every failure mode: bad evidence, mistaken reasoning, and a blind spot shared across a chain of verifiers stay real risks a contract does not by itself remove.
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:43:Contract-as-spec is a method for preserving that continuity. It makes a <Term id="feature-contract">**Feature Contract**</Term>, not a prompt, ticket, plan, or final summary, the binding document for the work. The contract states the intended outcome, defines what completion means, and names the evidence required to support it, plus who can accept the result. Specialists can interpret and execute the contract, but they do not silently redefine it.
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:48:**[Observed]** marks findings from a bounded landscape review or direct operating evidence. **[Proposed]** marks a method, interpretation, or product direction whose value should be validated in use. I did not invent this convention for the essay. It is the same instruction I give every agent I run, written down plainly: do not make things up; if you don't see it, don't say it.
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:63:- <Term id="evidence-obligations">**Evidence obligations:**</Term> What must be inspected, tested, reproduced, or independently verified?
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:64:- <Term id="decision-authority">**Decision authority:**</Term> Who or what may accept the evidence and close the work?
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:66:These elements bind the stages together. A specialist does not just receive a task; it receives a task whose relationship to the intended outcome is explicit. A verifier does not just review an output; it evaluates named claims against named evidence. A decision-maker does not just approve a summary; it accepts or rejects a contract with a visible evidentiary state.
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:92:A complete populated instance (claims bound to evidence, verifier status, a visible amendment, and the acceptance decision) is available as a [companion example](/essays/contract-as-spec-worked-example).
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:111:The conceptual core of contract-as-spec is a maturity model for evidence and authority. It distinguishes workflows that just produce outputs from workflows that can justify decisions.
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:129:The workflow separates claims from narrative and binds each consequential claim to supporting evidence.
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:135:A verifier tests whether the evidence genuinely supports the claim, whether contradictory evidence was considered, and whether the stated scope matches what was examined.
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:137:Verification is not the same as agreement. It records the status of a claim: supported, contradicted, unresolved, or limited by an evidence gap.
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:139:That distinction is also why test-based evaluation needs a stated boundary. SWE-bench evaluates models against issue-linked tests from real repositories, a valuable and demanding measure of whether a patch resolves a specified software problem.[^swebench] It is still evidence for that specified problem, not a blanket acceptance decision about every operational consequence a team may care about.
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:155:**[Observed]** Stages four through six are still frontier territory at organizational scale. They are not imaginary, though. As of 2026-08-25, we run a small version at home every day: three different LLM vendors and deterministic scripts operate under one contract boundary, with durable daily run records showing the handoffs. That is lived practice at modest scale, not evidence that the institutional problem is solved.
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:171:Can the planner point to the same acceptance criteria the verifier later evaluates? Can a claim be traced to its sources after the originating agent has disappeared? Does an approval identify exactly which evidence state was accepted? If an artifact is reused six months later, can its consumer determine which assumptions, validators, and contract version produced it? If a gate is deferred, can another operator resume it without reconstructing the decision from chat history?
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:175:**[Proposed, grounded in observed landscape coverage]** The meaningful differentiation of a governed agentic system lies in integration completeness rather than primitive technical novelty. Its value comes from closing the seams among intent, execution, evidence, verification, authority, and reuse.
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:191:Before specialist execution, a gate can ask whether the contract is sufficiently clear to begin. Before accepting research, it can ask whether important claims have evidence and verification states. Before operational change, it can ask whether the acceptance criteria and risk boundaries have been met. Before reuse, it can ask whether the capability includes enough provenance to behave predictably outside its original context.
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:205:This also changes the meaning of "blocked." A blocked agent is not just an agent that failed to continue; it can identify the exact contract obligation it cannot satisfy: unavailable evidence, ambiguous authority, contradictory acceptance criteria, or a task too large for the current specialist. That is useful state, and it lets the work be decomposed or escalated without erasing what has already been learned.
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:217:A reusable artifact should answer: Where did this capability come from? What contract does it satisfy? Which evidence supported its acceptance? Which version is running? What changed between versions? What assumptions travel with it? Who may promote or retire it?
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:231:A research team can return claims with provenance. An engineering team can turn accepted claims into implementation criteria. A security team can add verification obligations and decision boundaries. An operator can inspect the evidence state without replaying the entire execution. A future team can reproduce the decision even if the original tools no longer exist.
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:233:This does require discipline. Acceptance criteria must describe outcomes rather than activity, and evidence has to be durable enough to inspect later. Verification must be distinguishable from self-report. Decision authority must be explicit, and derived records need to identify their authoritative source.
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:235:But the method does not demand maximal ceremony. Teams can begin with a small contract containing intent, three or four acceptance criteria, required evidence, and one named decision authority. They can add claim-level verification and reusable artifacts as the consequences of the work increase.
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:239:I don't actually know whether anyone outside my own household would pay for this. The receipts in this essay show the mechanism operating in my home environment: specific handoffs, evidence states, and corrections I can point to; they don't prove a market, and I'm not going to pretend this essay settles that question. What I do have some confidence in, because I've watched it happen both ways, is what a chain of agents does without this discipline versus what it can do with it. The doubt is about who pays. The confidence is about what the mechanism does when you run it for real.
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:247:Contract-as-spec makes those transitions explicit. It binds intent to acceptance criteria, routes bounded work through specialists, validates completeness at meaningful gates, and preserves the evidence needed to inspect or reproduce the result.
src/content/posts/the-contract-is-the-work.mdx:323:Author: Nick Miethe. Thesis: human-originated. Evidence: operating observations plus a bounded internal landscape review (41 sources: 35 publicly linked primary sources and six non-public working materials; scope and caveats stated above). AI roles: research assistance, adversarial review, drafting. Human verification: final claim and source review by Nick. Research artifact: contract-as-spec landscape review, with its recovered source record listed above. Version: rev5. AI-involvement level: 3 (substantive synthesis and adversarial reasoning, thesis and evidence Nick's own operating observations).
src/content/stories/after-action-report-fable-5-one-shot-command-center-build.mdx:45:Before writing a line, the recon pass read the live tree and found an in-flight feature nobody had mentioned: APWR (the Agentic Work-Product Registry), living in its own worktree `awpr-exec` with 11 commits, 8 new registry tables (`source_artifact`, `work_products`, `completion_evidence`, `external_link`, `validation_run`, and more), migration 0005, and uncommitted frontend work. It overlapped with the planned v2 on exactly the surfaces that hurt most: enums, entities, the router registry, `routes.tsx`, `types/domain.ts`, and `styles/index.css`.
src/content/stories/after-action-report-fable-5-one-shot-command-center-build.mdx:51:Two slices shipped. **Phase-0**, the AOS Launcher on `/command-center`, is a YAML-backed read-only API (apps, layers, diagrams, decisions, health) plus React screens; its backend came back green on the first pass with 42 new tests, and the frontend fixer that cleared five test failures also found a real double-filter bug where toggling "show personal" did nothing. It carries the full 34-app catalog (8 core, 9 supporting, 17 peripheral), 1,532 lines of verbatim-from-catalog YAML with every dependency ID resolving. **v2**, the centerpiece, is the full Workspace Command Center: a carousel landing, an expanded command center with a five-mode focus pane, drag-and-drop with a persistent tabbed Carry Tray, a capture-to-inbox-to-execution flow with execution-packet panels and an evidence timeline, and an `@xyflow/react` canvas with typed edges. Its seed populates four real workspaces (including the Agentic OS one built from 17 catalog projects, 17 intent nodes, 46 backlog tasks, and 16 dependency edges). Tests grew from 508 to 800 on the backend and from 140 to 424-plus on the frontend.
src/content/stories/after-action-report-fable-5-one-shot-command-center-build.mdx:121:- **D2 (model reuse):** Item equals TreeNode (a superset via additive enums; a `lifecycle_pinned` shim guards the legacy status auto-derivation). Project equals Tree (plus `parent_tree_id`). Workspace extended in place. Typed links became an EdgeType extension (`informs`/`derives_from`), not a new table, following APWR Decision #2; `evidence_for`/`executed_by`/`documented_in` reuse APWR's `completion_evidence` and `external_link`. External runs extend `agent_runs` (`is_external`, `harness_type`, packet FK, ingestion events); the worker loop skips external runs; SSE dual-publishes to run and workspace channels. The only new tables: `tray_entries`, `captures`, `execution_packets`.
