src/content/stories/dev-stories-b2-enterprise-multi-tenancy.mdx:171:The cleanest forensic evidence of the parallel-execution claim sits in the Router Migration features. Router Migration v1 and v2 each ran 8 Opus sessions inside a roughly 24-hour window (March 29 through 30), covering 56 tasks between them. That's the "five agents in parallel" pattern materializing in the CCDash trace as overlapping session lineage, not as a marketing claim.
src/content/stories/dev-stories-b2-enterprise-multi-tenancy.mdx:174:The shape of the work is itself the evidence that orchestration overhead stayed manageable. 70 sessions and ~47.5 active execution hours compressed into an 18-day window, with the two Router Migration features overlapping inside a single 24-hour span, is not what serial coordination looks like. Parallel agent execution is feasible when the interface is locked first and file ownership is batched cleanly; the session density on these PRs is what that feasibility looks like in telemetry.
src/content/stories/dev-stories-b2-enterprise-multi-tenancy.mdx:204:As I argued in [SDLC Post 1](/essays/governed-agentic-sdlc-01-productivity-paradox), infrastructure is what lets productivity gains compound. This post is the evidence: with the right seams in place, AI agents turned a project that would otherwise occupy an entire backend team into a 12-week parallel execution with zero conflicts. The seams were the infrastructure. Not the agents. The seams.
src/content/stories/dev-stories-b2-enterprise-multi-tenancy.mdx:210:*This is a field note from the "I Let Claude Build My App" series, grounding the Governed Agentic SDLC claims with evidence from real ship dates, real metrics, and real trade-offs.*
